    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Kruisjassen developed in the eastern Netherlands as a simplified variant of the broader Jass family of games. The name refers to the cross-wise seating of partnerships. It emerged as a more accessible alternative to complex bidding games like Klaverjassen.',
      originCountries: [
        'Netherlands',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '19th Century: Jass games spread through the Netherlands from Swiss/French origins',
        'Early 1900s: Kruisjassen emerges as distinct simplified variant in eastern Netherlands',
        'Mid-20th Century: Becomes popular in cafe leagues and family settings',
        'Present: Remains popular in Gelderland and eastern regions',
      ],
      evolution: 'The game simplified the Jass format by removing bidding and meld announcements, focusing purely on trick-taking and point accumulation. This made it faster and more accessible to casual players.',
      cultural: 'Commonly played in Dutch cafes (bruine cafÃ©s) and at family gatherings. The straightforward rules make it a popular teaching game for introducing players to the Jass family.',
